t-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"500\" fill=\"#0B3B31\" pointer-events=\"none\"><tspan x=\"0\" y=\"0.0\">2 · Walking the Tetrahedron</tspan></text></a></g><g id=\"node-p5\" transform=\"translate(500,740)\" style=\"cursor: move; opacity: 1;\"><title>📌 The only method whose output is a disagreement. Independent assessments, then discuss green-vs-red splits.</title><rect x=\"-105\" y=\"-39\" width=\"210\" height=\"78\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#0E5A4B\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"101\" cy=\"-35\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"1\" pointer-events=\"none\"/><a class=\"internal\" data-title=\"5 · Collective Action Assessment\" href=\"/5--collective-action-assessment.html\"><text text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"500\" fill=\"#0B3B31\" pointer-events=\"none\"><tspan x=\"0\" y=\"-8.0\">5 · Collective Action</tspan><tspan x=\"0\" dy=\"16\">Assessment</tspan></text></a></g><g id=\"node-p3\" transform=\"translate(820,740)\" style=\"cursor: move; opacity: 1;\"><title>📌 For complex problems. 14 leverage points, then the numbers gate, then SPC.</title><rect x=\"-105\" y=\"-39\" width=\"210\" height=\"78\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#0E5A4B\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"101\" cy=\"-35\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"1\" pointer-events=\"none\"/><a class=\"internal\" data-title=\"3 · Shared Understanding to Experiment\" href=\"/3--shared-understanding-to-experiment.html\"><text text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"500\" fill=\"#0B3B31\" pointer-events=\"none\"><tspan x=\"0\" y=\"-8.0\">3 · Shared Understanding to</tspan><tspan x=\"0\" dy=\"16\">Experiment</tspan></text></a></g><g id=\"node-p4\" transform=\"translate(1140,740)\" style=\"cursor: move; opacity: 1;\"><title>📌 The 15 Ps. Ends in Posterity — the move from model to pattern, which writes a new page onto the shelf.</title><rect x=\"-105\" y=\"-39\" width=\"210\" height=\"78\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#0E5A4B\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"101\" cy=\"-35\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"1\" pointer-events=\"none\"/><a class=\"internal\" data-title=\"4 · Inspiration to Implementation\" href=\"/4--inspiration-to-implementation.html\"><text text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"500\" fill=\"#0B3B31\" pointer-events=\"none\"><tspan x=\"0\" y=\"-8.0\">4 · Inspiration to</tspan><tspan x=\"0\" dy=\"16\">Implementation</tspan></text></a></g></g>\n      <g id=\"triples-layer\"/>\n      <g id=\"drag-layer\"/>\n      <g id=\"eip-statement-layer\"/>\n      \n      <g id=\"opm-symbols-chart\"/>\n    </svg>"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "text": "## Two models govern the use of all the others",
      "id": "5700311c3c459f05"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "text": "> Beauty is the basis for using all the models. That is why I put it at the top along with the most general model of humans taking action together: the Social Action Tetrahedron. All the models below these two must be informed by these or they will lead to no good. — [[Models for Seeing Systems]]",
      "id": "8645cda6a2e70cbd"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "text": "This is a precondition, not a pathway. See [[The Two Governing Models]].",
      "id": "8d78c883aded92bc"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "text": "## One structure runs through everything below",
      "id": "64dc417702c5f3fd"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "text": "Under the two governing models, one structure appears in nearly every framework: the Six Nested Questions — When, Where, Why, Who, How, What.",
      "id": "d6dd9bc330fa11f9"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "text": "They are the ordered chain in [[Neighborhood Systems Patterns]]. They are the first node of [[Another Sequence of Tools]]. They are what [[Story Structure]] exists to answer in narrative form. They are the tag on every one of the [[15 Ps]]. They are the extension of Meadows, with Time and Place added to contextualize the twelve leverage points.",
      "id": "74b1cdd05a54bb05"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "text": "> The Story Structure diagram places the modeling tools not in a linear sequence but in a topology organized around the Six Questions. This is the most important organizational insight in the conversation. — [[RCN Modeling Theory]]",
      "id": "b30cbcf10f32525d"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "text": "So the shelves for the storefront were not invented. See [[The Six Nested Questions]].",
      "id": "2220abb592ac731c"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "text": "## The sequence is not a pipeline",
      "id": "0a9110a4833662d4"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "text": "> The sequence is not a pipeline. It is a progression of question types, and the tools are matched to questions. — [[RCN Modeling Theory]]",
      "id": "3467d67819fe1f9c"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "text": "A step is therefore never \"now open the Graph Tool\". A step is a question you are now ready to ask, with a tool named as the instrument that answers it. Every pathway below is written that way.",
      "id": "ae99a0b19d43ce93"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "text": "## The rest of it",
      "id": "c97e3b71958bc550"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "text": "- [[The Two Governing Models]] — the crown, and why nothing below it is neutral\n- [[The Six Nested Questions]] — the shelves, and the tools placed against them\n- [[Models and Patterns]] — the particular and the general, and the move between them\n- [[The Causal Ladder]] — where to go when lost\n- [[Requisite Variety]] — Ashby, and the rule under every tool choice\n- [[Four Kinds of Sequence]] — ontological, diagnostic, production, curricular\n- [[WHO Collective Action After Ostrom]] — the thinnest shelf, built out\n- [[Three Files One Relation Each]] — where skills, pathways and tools each live",
      "id": "e1f40f747bb8c260"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "text": "## The five pathways",
      "id": "1c395b6dd8cb69f4"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "text": "- [[Systemic Problem Solving]] — the router. It chooses the other four.\n- [[Walking the Tetrahedron]] — the crown used as a check\n- [[Collective Action Assessment]] — the only method whose output is a disagreement\n- [[Shared Understanding to Experiment]] — for genuinely complex situations\n- [[Inspiration to Implementation]] — the long arc, ending in a pattern",
      "id": "ea7a75ca0c834922"
    }
  ],
